# Working with dude

This project pins its own toolchain: `package.json` pins the
`@cubocicloide/dude` CLI **and** the `@cubocicloide/stack-airflow` stack, and
`dude.json` records the same pins plus the answers given at `dude init`. The
globally-installed **launcher** (`npm i -g @cubocicloide/dude-launcher`) makes
`dude <cmd>` run this project's pinned versions automatically.

```bash
npm install -g @cubocicloide/dude-launcher   # once per machine
pnpm install                     # provision the pinned toolchain
dude help                        # the live, resolved command catalog
```

## Everyday loop

```bash
dude up --build     # after changing requirements.txt or the Dockerfile
dude up             # otherwise (dags/ and plugins/ are bind-mounted — no rebuild)
dude logs airflow-scheduler
dude dag errors     # is everything importable?
dude lint           # conventions (AF rules — see .claude/rules/AF/)
dude cheatsheet     # this project at a glance; --format json for agents
dude test           # DAG integrity suite, inside the image
dude format         # ruff format + import sort
dude down
```

## Custom project commands

Drop a TypeScript file in `.dude/commands/` — it becomes `dude <filename>`
(see `.dude/commands/README.md` and the `hello.ts` example). Project commands
take precedence over stack commands, which take precedence over core ones.

## Project lint rules

`dude lint` also runs any rules you define under `.dude/lint/checks/` — the
rule code is derived from the path (`.dude/lint/checks/PRJ/001.ts` → `PRJ001`)
and the contract is the same one the stack's own checks use. See
`.dude/lint/checks/README.md` and the `PRJ/001.ts` example. Document a rule with
a Markdown file next to it (`PRJ/001.md`) and `dude explain PRJ001` serves it.
To disable a built-in rule, list its code in `dude.json`:

```json
{ "lint": { "disable": ["AF003"] } }
```

### Verifying your work mechanically

`dude lint` and `dude explain` are designed to be used together — the fastest
loop for anyone, human or coding agent, working in this project:

```bash
dude lint --format json     # what broke, where, and under which rule code
dude explain AF003          # why that rule exists and how to satisfy it
```

`--format json` writes nothing but the JSON document to stdout, so it pipes
cleanly (`dude lint --format json | jq '.diagnostics[].code'`). Exit codes are
the same as the human format: non-zero only when there is at least one error.
Run `dude explain` with no code to list every rule that applies here.

## Upgrading

```bash
dude upgrade             # bump CLI and/or stack pins interactively
pnpm install             # apply
```

`dude upgrade` moves the pins in `package.json` + `dude.json` in lockstep; it
does not rewrite scaffolded files. The Airflow **image** version is upgraded
separately — bump `AIRFLOW_VERSION` in `airflow/Dockerfile`, review
`requirements.txt` against the new constraints file, `dude up --build`{{#if withIac}},
and run `dude iac migrate --env <env>` after shipping (metadata schema){{/if}}.
